---
title: 'Financial Charts - Range Buttons'
enterprise: true
---

Range Buttons allow the user to easily navigate to specific time periods and ranges along the chart timeline.

## Range Buttons

{% chartExampleRunner title="Range Buttons" name="range-buttons" type="generated" /%}

The Range Buttons are enabled by default for Financial Charts. To hide them, set the `rangeButtons` chart option to `false`.

## Custom Buttons

It is possible to override the default buttons by providing an array of button objects.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Custom Range Buttons" name="custom-range-buttons" type="generated" /%}

Custom buttons can be defined through the `ranges` options object.

Where `MONTH` is a duration constant (e.g. `const MONTH = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000`):

```js format="snippet"
{
    ranges: {
        enabled: true,
        buttons: [
            {
                label: '6 Months',
                value: 6 * MONTH,
            },
            {
                label: '12 Months',
                value: 12 * MONTH,
            },
            {
                label: 'February',
                value: [new Date(2023, 1, 1), new Date(2023, 2, 1)],
            },
            {
                label: 'All Data',
                value: (start, end, windowStart, windowEnd) => [start, end],
            },
        ],
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

-   `label` specifies the text displayed on the button.
-   `value` determines the time range the button applies:
    -   For "6 Months" and "12 Months", it sets the range to the past 6 or 12 months, respectively.
    -   For "February", it sets the range to February 2023, from February 1 to March 1.
    -   For "All Data", it dynamically sets the range to include all available data from the start to the end date. The function receives four parameters: the full data `start` and `end` dates, and the current `windowStart` and `windowEnd` dates.

## Position

The position of the range buttons on the chart can be configured using the `position` property. Supported values are `'top-left'`, `'top'`, `'top-right'`, `'bottom-left'`, `'bottom'`, and `'bottom-right'`. The default is `'top-right'`.

```js format="snippet"
{
    ranges: {
        position: 'bottom-left',
    },
}
```

## Dropdown

When there are many range buttons, they can be swapped for a dropdown menu. This behaviour is controlled by the `dropdown.visible` property, which accepts `'auto'`, `'always'`, or `'never'`. The default is `'auto'`, which automatically switches to a dropdown when space is limited.

```js format="snippet"
{
    ranges: {
        dropdown: {
            visible: 'always',
        },
    },
}
```

## Out of Range Buttons

By default, buttons whose target range falls outside the available data are disabled. Set `enableOutOfRange` to `true` to keep these buttons enabled regardless.

```js format="snippet"
{
    ranges: {
        enableOutOfRange: true,
    },
}
```

